you know it's true the holidays can  really be stressful for just about  anyone but if you are caring for someone  with alzheimer's or dementia you  probably feel that stress year round  well caregivers are so often busy  meeting they are so busy meeting the  needs of their loved one that they  neglect their own physical and even  mental health i met a devoted wife who  ignored signs of serious heart problems  until it was almost too late  i knew there was a problem i ignored it  and i'm i think typical of most  caregivers coco and bob hickman spent  much of their 35-year marriage traveling  and chasing adventure but the past 10  years have been a journey the fun-loving  partners never planned on coco has  become bob's full-time caregiver as he's  declined with lewy body dementia we call  him louie third person refer to him that  way  not good  the couple tries to maintain their sense  of humor through this debilitating  disease  bob's vivid hallucinations forced him to  hand over the car keys travel nowadays  consists of routine doctor visits coco  handles his medications his meals bob's  failing memory and all of the household  chores the emotional and physical strain  is constant caregiving is such a time  intensive process and particularly in  the later stages of the disease it truly  becomes a 24 hour seven day a week job  and so as a result caregivers have less  and less time for maintaining their own  health that became all too real for coco  the couple recently moved from las vegas  to washington state to be near family in  case of an emergency and she's glad they  did i had  an issue where i had to be life flighted  to seattle  for heart issues  thankfully they kept me alive until i  got there coco now has a pacemaker but  the outcome could have been very  different dr lucille carrier is a  clinical health psychologist at the lou  rouevo center for brain health in las  vegas she urges caregivers to speak up  and we want you to reach out you may not  know what your needs are you know as  professionals let us help you you know  or talk to another caregiver who's in a  similar place in the journey  bob participates in a weekly virtual  dementia music class giving his wife  some time to herself and coco has joined  a valuable caregiver support group  social connection human connection at  its basic form  is such a vital component along the  caregiver journey to reduce those  feelings of isolation  to reduce burden strain coco says she  now realizes that her own well-being  ensures that she'll be there for bob as  long as he needs her i can't overstate  that because if you don't take care of  yourself you can't take care of them  well coco  also recently just took a much-needed  weekend away with her sister for a break  and a change of scenery dr carrier says  respite for caregivers is incredibly  important here's what else she suggests  reach out to family friends or neighbors  for help so that you can take those  breaks when you need them  make a care plan for yourself along with  your loved one if you're staying healthy  then you'll be able to keep them healthy  as well  connect with people either through a  group or a friend for conversation allow  yourself time to vent and get out that  frustration  stay informed of your loved one's  prognosis so that you can plan ahead and  overcome the fear of the unknown  and also work through your grief the  grief of the loss of your loved one  all great information
 

